2026-11-05 Ticket grew to about 57 members by copying what its collaborators own: flattened dicts of each fidelity's state, actions, allowed actions, reports and violation counts; *_name copies of its column and parent; its session's workpath, practice roots and session file; a TicketFiles helper and a Workspace bag. The model drawio was then redrawn from that code, so the model followed the bloat instead of the code following the model.
Correction: do-not-invent-parallel-object-models (clean_engineering.md shared rules), which already covered this; validate passed it because it judged each file alone. The rule now names the small copies and says to read the owning class of every field the change adds. Ticket works with its Session, its FidelityProgress and the Board, and hands callers those objects; each owner carries its own data and tools (session.practices[key].fidelities[name].files(), progress.report(), board.activate(ticket)). Change the model first, then make the code match it.

2026-11-12 Generate at stories-acceptance-tests for Maintain Tickets On Board wrote the shared stories and the domain and server specs, and no screen story: maintain-tickets.story.ui.spec.ts and organize-ticket-hierarchy.story.ui.spec.ts did not exist, and See Work's screen spec had no describe for Expand Ticket From Empty Area. The shared file even said Display Child Tickets In Parent and Expand Parent Ticket were "left to the board app's TypeScript specs", which nobody wrote; Detach Child Ticket was built only in Python, so no person could do it on the board. Validate passed it because LERN called the Playwright file optional.
Correction: the LERN pattern (`playwright-tests-browser-interaction`, the test layout and generate step 3 in lern_domain_driven.md) now requires a screen story for every story a person does on a screen: a describe named as the story, a test per scenario, driving the real page. Only a system or technical story has none. Write the screen story with the shared story, and build the screen it drives.
